---
// AdwWidget — the documentation block for a single Adwaita component.
//
// Renders a COLUMN OF WINDOWS, one per KIND of implementation, because the kind
// is the distinction a reader cannot recover on their own:
//
//   1. THE WIDGET — the real component, painted by @gjsify/adwaita-web custom
//      elements on an Adwaita "view" surface. No screenshots: the browser runs it,
//      the way the official Libadwaita widget gallery shows one, and it follows the
//      site's light/dark toggle.
//   2. GJS — `@girs` GObject-Introspection TypeScript and the matching Blueprint
//      declaration: two FILES of one program, against the REAL libadwaita widget.
//   3. UI FRAMEWORKS — the same widget reached through an ELEMENT MODEL instead of
//      the GObject API: the markup the first window is painted from, the three
//      `@gjsify/gtk-host` dialects, and the NativeScript XML template.
//
// It used to be ONE window with all of those as sibling tabs, and siblings cannot
// say any of it: `gjs`/`blueprint` ARE libadwaita, `preview` and `nativescript` are
// REIMPLEMENTATIONS of it, and a framework tab drives the real one. A window can say
// it, in its title. {@link WINDOWS} is that model and it is the one place a port is
// declared — `scripts/check-website-adwaita-gallery.mjs` holds it against the pages
// in both directions, and refuses a window no page fills.
//
// THE FIRST WINDOW HAS NO TABS. It carried Preview | HTML | Attributes, and a tab
// bar over a running widget offers the reader a choice between the widget and its
// sources — which reads as if the widget were one of them. It is not a source, so it
// gets a window and nothing else in it. Its markup IS a source, and it is now a tab
// of the window that holds every other element-model rendering of the same widget,
// labelled "HTML Web Components": that pane is a reimplementation driven by a
// framework-ish element model, which is what that window is about.
//
// THE `nativescript` FENCE IS A CORPUS, NOT A TAB — see {@link CORPUS_SLOTS}. The end
// state of convergence (ADR 0034) is that a block's NativeScript program IS its GJS
// program, byte for byte. Rendering both is the redundancy a reader noticed. So the
// fence stays AUTHORED on every page and stays arm 12's input: that arm proves it is
// the same text, and where it is not, the recorded reason is what a reader needs —
// which this window model already renders, through the refusal pane of the group the
// port's own template belongs to.
//
// THE ATTRIBUTE TAB IS GONE. It listed what `observedAttributes` names beside what
// the block's preview writes, which is what a reader PASSES and not what any of it
// MEANS — too little to hold a pane on 39 blocks. The three incidents it was carrying
// are in docs/code-anti-patterns.md (§ "A documentation surface written by hand, once
// per page" and § "A `:global()` selector inside an `is:global` block"), and the
// scanner it fed still runs: `check-website-attr-samples.mjs` holds every preview
// fence against the element it names.
//
// ONE CLAIM THE CHROME NO LONGER MAKES, load-bearing, still said. The GJS window's
// tab was once labelled "GJS · Node · Bun · Deno" and its window "Native TypeScript",
// both to avoid naming one runtime: naming GJS made the pane read as "the GJS
// variant", i.e. as if the other three needed a different program. They do not, and
// that is the claim the whole site rests on. It is now made ONCE, on the pages that
// are about runtimes (getting-started, runtimes, the frameworks index), instead of in
// an intro paragraph under all 40 blocks and again in a window subtitle. So the window
// can be titled for what it runs, which is the shortest true name it has.
//
// THE `react-native` TAB IS GONE AND ITS REASONING IS NOT. It was filled on 3 blocks
// of 40, all on `layout.mdx`, against 28 blocks carrying a `react` snippet: a footnote
// holding a window pane. What those three showed — an Adwaita element with React
// Native children, and the token step the children need — `layout.mdx` now says once
// in prose, and `frameworks/react-native.mdx` shows in full.
//
// Its LABEL carried a qualifier ("React Native on GTK") because a plain "React Native"
// standing beside NativeScript reads as the port that runs on a phone, which is not
// what that pane rendered. That reason once rested on there being no Adwaita widget
// set built out of React Native primitives; #1380 wrote one
// (`@gjsify/adwaita-react-native`) and the conclusion survived on other grounds, since
// what was at stake is what THAT PANE showed and not what exists elsewhere in the
// tree. Both halves are written down where a reader meets them: the direction argument
// in `frameworks/react-native.mdx` § "What this is not", the general shape in
// docs/code-anti-patterns.md § "A rule whose premise died and whose conclusion did
// not". Read those before putting the tab back.
//
// So the header bar carries a title and nothing else. Whatever a header bar sits on
// is what the reader takes the window to be about: it once held the widget TITLE over
// a pane of code, and the title read as belonging to the code. The window carrying
// the widget's title is therefore the one that RUNS the widget, and every other
// window is titled for its kind.
//
// ONE SOURCE FOR THE MARKUP. The `preview` slot is a fenced ```html block: the first
// window MOUNTS it and the frameworks window SHOWS it (Expressive Code, exactly as
// authored), from the same bytes.
// It used to be a fragment of MDX markup with a second, hand-kept `web` fence
// beside it saying the same thing — measured across the 40 blocks, 17 were
// byte-identical and 23 had already drifted, and nothing checked them. The drift
// was not cosmetic: `Adw.ToolbarView`'s preview carried a sizing wrapper its
// snippet omitted, `Adw.Carousel`'s snippet was a different document from the one
// the page renders, and eight rows previewed inside an `<adw-preferences-group>`
// their snippet dropped. A reader copying the tab often did not get the widget
// above it. One fence renders and is shown, so there is no second copy to keep.
//
// WHICH IS ALSO WHERE AN ATTRIBUTE'S MEANING NOW LIVES. An HTML comment is inert in the
// DOM, so a gloss written into that one fence is shown on the HTML tab, copied with the
// markup, and mounts as nothing. `scripts/generate-adwaita-attribute-comments.mjs` writes
// one per attribute whose GIR doc says something the attribute's own NAME does not — 27
// of the 107 this gallery sets, measured, with the other 80 accounted for in the same run
// (ADR 0052). It replaces a pane that listed every `observedAttributes` name beside the
// value this preview passes, which described what the fence beside it already showed.
//
// Feed the rest as named MDX slots (one fenced code block each). A tab appears
// only where its slot was given, and a WINDOW only where at least one of its panes
// filled, so a port that cannot express THIS widget simply has no tab, and a kind
// with nothing to say has no window:
//
//   <AdwWidget title="Adw.SwitchRow">
//     <Fragment slot="preview">```html …```</Fragment>
//     <Fragment slot="gjs">```ts …```</Fragment>
//     <Fragment slot="blueprint">```blueprint …```</Fragment>
//     <Fragment slot="nativescript">```ts …```</Fragment>
//   </AdwWidget>

interface Props {
    /** The widget, e.g. "Adw.SwitchRow". Titles the live-preview window. */
    title?: string;
    /**
     * Padding around the preview widget. Use "flush" for widgets that own their
     * full surface (navigation views, status pages, toolbar views) so they sit
     * edge-to-edge; the default "comfortable" pads small widgets (rows, buttons).
     */
    padding?: 'comfortable' | 'flush';
    /** When set, the preview panel scrolls horizontally instead of clipping wide widgets. */
    scroll?: boolean;
    /**
     * Inline CSS for the preview STAGE — the box the widget is mounted into.
     *
     * This is preview-only scaffolding, and it is a prop precisely so that it
     * cannot be anything else. A widget that fills a window (a toolbar view, a
     * split view, a dialog) has no height of its own, so the gallery gives it one;
     * that height is a property of this PANEL, not of the widget, and every line
     * of it that sat in the markup was a line the reader copied and did not need.
     * Sixteen blocks wrapped their widget in a `<div style="height: …">` for it —
     * five then repeated the wrapper in their snippet and eleven did not, which is
     * the whole drift in one habit.
     *
     * Taken VERBATIM from the wrapper it replaces (`height: 340px; width: 100%;
     * display: flex; overflow: hidden;`) so the computed layout is unchanged: it
     * is an inline style on the element that wrapper's parent already was.
     */
    stage?: string;
    /**
     * Canonical upstream reference for this widget. Omit to derive it from an
     * `Adw.*` / `Gtk.*` `title` (libadwaita / GTK 4 class docs); pass a URL for a
     * title that isn't a single class, or `false` to suppress the link.
     */
    docHref?: string | false;
}

import {
    ADWAITA_FRAMEWORK_REFUSALS,
    ADWAITA_FRAMEWORK_SNIPPETS,
    type FrameworkDialect,
} from '../data/adwaita-framework-snippets';
import {
    ADWAITA_NATIVESCRIPT_REFUSALS,
    ADWAITA_NATIVESCRIPT_TEMPLATES,
} from '../data/adwaita-nativescript-templates';
import AdwWidgetWindow from './AdwWidgetWindow.astro';
import type { WidgetPane } from './widget-panes';

const { title = 'Code', padding = 'comfortable', scroll = false, stage, docHref } = Astro.props;

// Derive the upstream class-reference URL from a `Namespace.ClassName` title, so
// each widget links to its canonical docs (the sources this gallery documents).
const REF_BASES: Record<string, string> = {
    Adw: 'https://gnome.pages.gitlab.gnome.org/libadwaita/doc/main/class.',
    Gtk: 'https://docs.gtk.org/gtk4/class.',
};
const deriveRef = (t: string): string | null => {
    const m = /^(Adw|Gtk)\.([A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9]*)$/.exec(t);
    return m ? `${REF_BASES[m[1]]}${m[2]}.html` : null;
};
const refHref = docHref === false ? null : (docHref ?? deriveRef(title));

/**
 * The framework panes: Solid, Vue and React, rendered from
 * `data/adwaita-framework-snippets.ts` rather than written per page.
 *
 * Not slots — no page provides them and no page should, because the snippet a page
 * would carry is a COPY. Each one here was compiled and run first:
 * `showcases/gtk/adwaita-gallery-{solid,vue,react}` build this exact markup and
 * assert the resulting widget tree against GTK with the diagnostics gate on, and
 * `scripts/generate-adwaita-framework-snippets.mjs` extracts them from those
 * programs. That data file has existed and been gated since it landed; until this
 * component read it, nothing rendered it — 40 blocks' worth of generated, checked,
 * invisible snippets.
 *
 * The order is the data file's own, and it puts the three host adapters together:
 * they are one element model (`@gjsify/gtk-host`) behind three renderer APIs.
 */
const FRAMEWORK_PANES: readonly { id: FrameworkDialect; label: string; lang: string }[] = [
    { id: 'solid', label: 'Solid', lang: 'tsx' },
    { id: 'vue', label: 'Vue', lang: 'vue' },
    { id: 'react', label: 'React', lang: 'tsx' },
];

/**
 * The NativeScript XML template, from `data/adwaita-nativescript-templates.ts`.
 *
 * A SLOT until #1502, and filled on four blocks of forty — which is the shape this
 * gallery has been told twice that it cannot keep: written per page, a snippet is a
 * copy, and thirty-six of them were simply never written. The generator behind this
 * data emits the same bytes into `showcases/dom/adwaita-gallery-nativescript`, which
 * inflates each one through NativeScript's own `Builder` on an Android device and
 * asserts the view tree it built.
 *
 * Labelled for the PORT and not for the file format, because it is the only pane of
 * this window that runs on a phone and that is the fact a reader is looking for.
 * Its sibling TypeScript pane is a corpus now ({@link CORPUS_SLOTS}), so "XML" no
 * longer distinguishes it from anything on screen.
 */
const NATIVESCRIPT_PANES: readonly { id: string; label: string; lang: string }[] = [
    { id: 'nativescript-xml', label: 'NativeScript', lang: 'xml' },
];

/**
 * A GROUP of data panes and the recorded reason that stands in for them.
 *
 * Two of them share the frameworks window, and they partition INDEPENDENTLY: 28
 * blocks have a framework tree and 12 a reason, 28 have an XML template and 12 a
 * reason, and the two sets of 12 are not the same 12 (`Gtk.DropDown` has a tree and
 * no template; `Adw.PreferencesDialog` the reverse). One `code`/`refusal` pair per
 * window could not express that: a block with a template and no tree would show the
 * template and no framework reason, which is the silently-absent tab both refusal
 * maps exist to prevent.
 */
type PaneGroup = {
    code: readonly { id: string; label: string; lang: string }[];
    codeOf: (widget: string, pane: string) => string | undefined;
    refusalOf: (widget: string) => string | undefined;
    /** The pane the reason renders as. Its `pane` id has to be unique across ALL windows. */
    refusal: { pane: string; label: string; missing: string; note: string };
};

const FRAMEWORK_GROUP: PaneGroup = {
    code: FRAMEWORK_PANES,
    codeOf: (widget, pane) => ADWAITA_FRAMEWORK_SNIPPETS[widget]?.[pane as FrameworkDialect],
    refusalOf: (widget) => ADWAITA_FRAMEWORK_REFUSALS[widget],
    refusal: {
        pane: 'why-not',
        label: 'Why not',
        missing: 'Solid, Vue or React snippet',
        note:
            'Every snippet in this gallery was compiled and run before it was written down. Where a widget is ' +
            'built imperatively rather than declared — a Gio.MenuModel, a dialog opened with present(), a ' +
            'container with no child policy — there is nothing for the three dialects to differ about, so the ' +
            'reason is recorded here rather than the tab being quietly absent.',
    },
};

const NATIVESCRIPT_GROUP: PaneGroup = {
    // The XML template is a FILE of a program whose other file is the `nativescript`
    // corpus fence: NativeScript resolves `<adw:Clamp>` by loading the module the
    // template's `xmlns` names — one per library, `~/adw` and `~/gtk`, so the prefix
    // says which one a widget belongs to (ADR 0034 clause 2) — and the TypeScript is
    // the loader that mounts the template and does what markup cannot say.
    //
    // DATA and not a slot, and the two are not interchangeable here: an XML template
    // is the one dialect where "it renders" cannot be read off the text.
    // NativeScript's Builder assigns an attribute verbatim (`open="false"` opened a
    // dialog) and `LayoutBase` adds a child a widget cannot place anyway (a header
    // bar painted over the content). Both at exit 0. Only a run says which.
    code: NATIVESCRIPT_PANES,
    codeOf: (widget) => ADWAITA_NATIVESCRIPT_TEMPLATES[widget],
    refusalOf: (widget) => ADWAITA_NATIVESCRIPT_REFUSALS[widget],
    refusal: {
        pane: 'why-no-xml',
        // NOT "Why not", which is what both refusals rendered as while they sat in
        // windows of their own. They share a window now, and two tabs reading "Why
        // not" beside each other say nothing about which port is being refused.
        label: 'Why no XML',
        missing: 'NativeScript XML template',
        note:
            'NativeScript’s Builder reaches a widget through an attribute, which is always a string, and ' +
            'through a child, which lands only where the widget declares a slot for it. A property that is an ' +
            'array of options or a reference to another view fits through neither, so such a widget is built ' +
            'from TypeScript instead.',
    },
};

/**
 * The slot whose fence is BOTH mounted and shown: the live pane clones its bytes, and
 * the frameworks window's first tab renders it as code.
 *
 * A named constant because the two uses are now in different WINDOWS, so nothing else
 * in this file puts them side by side.
 */
const MARKUP_SLOT = 'preview';

/**
 * The one slot that is an OVERRIDE rather than a tab: markup shown in place of
 * {@link MARKUP_SLOT}'s own fence.
 *
 * It exists for a single widget and the gate holds it there with its reason:
 * `<adw-toast-overlay>` has no declarative toast child — `addToast()` is the whole
 * API — so the markup that PAINTS a toast in a preview is the overlay's internal
 * DOM, which is the one thing a reader must not copy. Everywhere else the fence
 * that renders is the fence that is shown, and there is no second copy to keep.
 */
const MARKUP_OVERRIDE = 'web';

/**
 * Slots a page AUTHORS that no window renders, with what reads each one instead.
 *
 * A CATEGORY and not an exemption list, because Astro drops an unmatched slot in
 * silence: without a name for it, a fence that is deliberately unrendered and a
 * fence that is unrendered by a typo look identical to arm 5 of
 * `check-website-adwaita-gallery.mjs`. So arm 6 refuses a corpus slot NO ARM READS,
 * and derives the reading arms from their own source rather than from a second list —
 * otherwise this becomes the hole a slot can be hidden in.
 *
 * `nativescript`: the end state of ADR 0034's convergence is that a block's
 * NativeScript program IS its GJS program, byte for byte. Rendering both is the
 * redundancy a reader noticed. So the fence is a CORPUS: arm 12 holds it against the
 * `gjs` fence of the same block and prints the distance, and where the two are not
 * one text the recorded reason is what a reader needs — which the frameworks window
 * already renders, as the NativeScript group's refusal pane. Deleting the fence would
 * delete the mechanism pushing the port to spell things the way GJS spells them;
 * `check-doc-fences.mjs` holds every property write in it against the port's source
 * as well.
 */
const CORPUS_SLOTS: Record<string, string> = {
    nativescript:
        'held against the `gjs` fence of the same block by arm 12 of ' +
        'scripts/check-website-adwaita-gallery.mjs, which prints how far apart the two are',
};

/**
 * The windows, in order, and every port this component renders.
 *
 * A window says what KIND it is in its TITLE, and nothing else: the claims that used
 * to sit under it as a subtitle are on the page (see the header). `live` marks the one
 * window that runs the widget.
 */
const WINDOWS: readonly {
    id: string;
    title: string | null;
    live?: boolean;
    tabs: readonly { slot: string; label: string }[];
    groups: readonly PaneGroup[];
}[] = [
    {
        id: 'preview',
        // No title of its own: this window takes the WIDGET's, because it is the
        // one that runs the widget. See the header — a header bar names whatever
        // it sits on, and this one sits on the running component.
        title: null,
        // The ONLY window with no tabs and no groups, and the only one whose pane
        // the component provides rather than a page. Its single pane is why it has
        // no tab bar; arm 9 holds both halves — that this window is FIRST, so the
        // reader meets the widget before any source, and that AdwWidgetWindow mounts
        // it outside its tab view, so the widget is not offered as one tab among the
        // sources again.
        live: true,
        tabs: [],
        groups: [],
    },
    {
        id: 'gjs',
        // The runtime, which is the shortest true name this window has. It was
        // "Native TypeScript" — titled for its AXIS rather than for GJS — while
        // every gallery page's intro carried the four runtimes that axis is about;
        // that sentence is now made once on the site's runtime pages, so the title
        // no longer has to avoid the word. See the header for why the four-runtime
        // claim is load-bearing and where it lives.
        //
        // The TITLE is the join with the pages: every gallery page's intro
        // enumerates the windows by these exact strings, so renaming one here and
        // nowhere else leaves nine pages naming a window that does not exist — which
        // is what happened, and is what arm 10 of
        // `scripts/check-website-adwaita-gallery.mjs` now refuses.
        title: 'GJS',
        tabs: [
            { slot: 'gjs', label: 'TypeScript' },
            // Blueprint is a FILE of the same program, which is why it is a tab here
            // and not a window of its own: a `.blp` is a widget tree and nothing
            // else, and the TypeScript beside it is the loader that mounts it.
            // Standing alone it read as a fourth complete program, which it is not.
            { slot: 'blueprint', label: 'Blueprint' },
        ],
        groups: [],
    },
    {
        id: 'frameworks',
        // Every rendering of this widget that goes through an ELEMENT MODEL rather
        // than through the GObject API: markup, three host dialects, and the XML
        // template.
        //
        // A `react-native` tab stood here until it was measured: 3 blocks of 40, all
        // on one page. See the header for what it claimed and where that claim
        // lives now, and read that before adding it back. The XML template is the
        // only pane under this title that runs on a phone, which is why it is
        // labelled for the port.
        title: 'UI frameworks',
        tabs: [
            // The markup the FIRST window is painted from, shown here because a
            // custom-element tree is an element model like the three below it. Its
            // fence is read for the mount whether or not this tab renders, so arm 9
            // holds that the fence a reader can copy is actually shown somewhere.
            { slot: MARKUP_SLOT, label: 'HTML Web Components' },
        ],
        // Filled on all 40 blocks, snippet or recorded reason — see {@link PaneGroup}.
        groups: [FRAMEWORK_GROUP, NATIVESCRIPT_GROUP],
    },
];

/** U+007F — Expressive Code's own stand-in for a newline inside `data-code`. */
const EC_NEWLINE = String.fromCharCode(0x7f);

/**
 * Expressive Code hands the raw source back on the copy button's `data-code`, with
 * newlines as U+007F (its own copy script does the same `replace`). That is how the
 * live preview gets the fence's bytes: rendered once, shown as code and mounted as
 * markup.
 *
 * Only NUMERIC entities are decoded, in one pass. EC escapes `&` as `&#x26;` and
 * leaves `<` raw, so `&#x26;lt;` is a literal `&lt;` in the source — decoding named
 * entities as well would turn it into `<` and quietly rewrite the author's markup.
 * `String.replace` never rescans what it substitutes, which is what makes one pass
 * exact.
 */
const decodeEcCode = (html: string): string | null => {
    const found = [...html.matchAll(/data-code="([^"]*)"/g)];
    if (found.length !== 1) return null;
    return found[0][1]
        .replaceAll(EC_NEWLINE, '\n')
        .replaceAll(/&#x([0-9a-fA-F]+);/g, (_m, hex: string) => String.fromCodePoint(Number.parseInt(hex, 16)))
        .replaceAll(/&#(\d+);/g, (_m, dec: string) => String.fromCodePoint(Number(dec)));
};

/**
 * The pane id of the LIVE PREVIEW in `data-impls`.
 *
 * Not a slot: no page provides it, the component is it — which is why it names no
 * slot in {@link WINDOWS}, where every tab is a port a page has to fill. It joins the
 * same list as the slots because that list is POSITIONAL, and it has to differ from
 * every slot name for the reason the collision throws below record.
 */
const LIVE_PANE = 'live';

/**
 * The panes that say why a block has NO snippet in a group's dialect.
 *
 * Each group names its own pane id, and they now share a window, so the requirement
 * is sharper than it was: `data-impls` is looked up by NAME, and one shared `why-not`
 * would put two entries reading the same id in ONE list, where the first answers for
 * both.
 */
const REFUSAL_PANES = WINDOWS.flatMap((win) => win.groups.map((group) => group.refusal.pane));

// That requirement, held rather than stated. `data-impls` is POSITIONAL and the
// script below looks a pane up by name with `indexOf`, so a pane id equal to a slot
// name gives one window two entries reading the same id and the first one answers for
// both: the colliding tab could never be the remembered choice, and picking it would
// move every OTHER window to whatever sits at that position. Nothing else notices —
// the ids never leave `data-impls`, so the site builds, every tab renders, and every
// gate stays green. The frameworks window is where this became likely: it mixes slot
// tabs with data panes and refusal panes in one list, so a slot named `solid` or
// `react` is an in-window collision rather than a hypothetical.
const OWN_PANES = [
    LIVE_PANE,
    ...REFUSAL_PANES,
    ...FRAMEWORK_PANES.map((pane) => pane.id),
    ...NATIVESCRIPT_PANES.map((pane) => pane.id),
];
const RENDERED_SLOTS = WINDOWS.flatMap((win) => win.tabs.map((tab) => tab.slot));
const collidingSlot = RENDERED_SLOTS.find((slot) => OWN_PANES.includes(slot));
if (collidingSlot !== undefined) {
    throw new Error(
        `AdwWidget: "${collidingSlot}" is both a tab slot and one of this component's own pane ids ` +
            `(${OWN_PANES.join(', ')}). The pane ids in \`data-impls\` are positional and looked up by ` +
            'name, so no two panes of one window can share an id.',
    );
}

// The OTHER half of that requirement, and it was stated and not held. Two windows
// sharing a pane id is the same defect as a slot colliding with one, one window over:
// the stored choice is a bare NAME, `applyPreference` writes it into EVERY window whose
// `data-impls` contains it, and a reader who picked the frameworks window's reason would
// land on another window's reason on the next block. {@link REFUSAL_PANES} is exactly
// where that becomes tempting — one `why-not` for both refusal maps reads like
// deduplication — and nothing downstream would notice, because a duplicate id still
// renders, still builds, and still leaves every gate green.
const duplicatePane = OWN_PANES.find((id, index) => OWN_PANES.indexOf(id) !== index);
if (duplicatePane !== undefined) {
    throw new Error(
        `AdwWidget: "${duplicatePane}" is declared as a pane id twice (${OWN_PANES.join(', ')}). ` +
            'A stored implementation choice is a bare name, so two windows sharing an id makes one of ' +
            "them answer for the other's panes. Give each window its own.",
    );
}

// A CORPUS slot is one no window renders, so a slot in both lists is a contradiction
// rather than a duplicate: the fence would render as a tab while every reader of
// {@link CORPUS_SLOTS} is told it does not, and arm 6 of the gate would look for an
// arm reading a slot the page can already see. Held here because this is the file
// where both lists are authored.
const renderedCorpusSlot = RENDERED_SLOTS.find((slot) => Object.hasOwn(CORPUS_SLOTS, slot));
if (renderedCorpusSlot !== undefined) {
    throw new Error(
        `AdwWidget: "${renderedCorpusSlot}" is declared in CORPUS_SLOTS and rendered as a tab of a ` +
            'window. A corpus slot is authored and deliberately NOT rendered; drop it from one list.',
    );
}

// The block's markup, read ONCE and before any window: it belongs to the BLOCK rather
// than to a window now that the pane that mounts it and the tab that shows it are in
// two different ones. It used to be read as a side effect of rendering the first
// window's first tab, which only worked while those were the same window.
const markupHtml = Astro.slots.has(MARKUP_SLOT) ? await Astro.slots.render(MARKUP_SLOT) : null;
const previewMarkup = markupHtml === null ? null : decodeEcCode(markupHtml);
// Loud, at build time, never at runtime: without the source there is no preview, and
// a silently empty stage on 40 blocks is exactly the failure this component was
// restructured to end.
//
// EMPTY counts as without. An empty ```html fence decodes to '' and not to null, so it
// walked past a `=== null` guard and mounted nothing: measured by emptying one fence —
// the site built at exit 0, this gate and `check-doc-fences.mjs` both stayed green, and
// /adwaita/controls/ shipped a "Preview" tab over an empty stage. That is the blank page
// at exit 0 with zero diagnostics that the throw exists to prevent, so it has to cover
// the blank one too.
if (markupHtml !== null && (previewMarkup === null || previewMarkup.trim() === '')) {
    throw new Error(
        `AdwWidget(${title}): the preview slot rendered no single NON-EMPTY Expressive Code ` +
            'block to read its markup back from. It has to hold exactly one fenced ```html ' +
            'block, with markup in it.',
    );
}

/**
 * One window's panes, in RENDER order, which is also `data-impls` order.
 *
 * ONE array for both, and that is the point: the order used to live twice — once as
 * the sequence of expressions in `AdwWidgetWindow`'s template and once as a
 * hand-assembled `impls` string here — with a comment in each file asking the next
 * editor to keep them in step. A stored preference is resolved by POSITION against
 * that string, so the two disagreeing sends a reader who picked one pane to a
 * different one, and nothing downstream can tell.
 */
const rendered: { id: string; title: string; panes: WidgetPane[] }[] = [];

for (const spec of WINDOWS) {
    const panes: WidgetPane[] = [];
    if (spec.live === true && previewMarkup !== null) {
        panes.push({ id: LIVE_PANE, kind: 'live', markup: previewMarkup });
    }
    for (const tab of spec.tabs) {
        if (!Astro.slots.has(tab.slot)) continue;
        let html: string;
        if (tab.slot === MARKUP_SLOT) {
            // The one override: the markup a reader should copy, where the fence that
            // renders is not it.
            html = Astro.slots.has(MARKUP_OVERRIDE) ? await Astro.slots.render(MARKUP_OVERRIDE) : (markupHtml ?? '');
        } else {
            html = await Astro.slots.render(tab.slot);
        }
        panes.push({ id: tab.slot, kind: 'slot', label: tab.label, html });
    }
    // The data panes of each group, looked up by the block's own title — the same key
    // the generator wrote. A block whose entry is missing a dialect shows the dialects
    // it has, because a pane rendering an empty fence is worse than an absent one; a
    // block with no entry at all gets the RECORDED REASON, never silence. The two maps
    // partition the gallery per group, and a block in NEITHER map of a group leaves
    // that group's pane absent, which arm 4 (frameworks) and arm 7 (NativeScript) of
    // `check-generated-website-data.mjs` are what refuse.
    for (const group of spec.groups) {
        const code = group.code
            .map((pane) => ({
                id: pane.id,
                kind: 'code' as const,
                label: pane.label,
                lang: pane.lang,
                source: group.codeOf(title, pane.id) ?? '',
            }))
            .filter((pane) => pane.source !== '');
        if (code.length > 0) {
            panes.push(...code);
            continue;
        }
        const reason = group.refusalOf(title);
        if (reason === undefined) continue;
        panes.push({
            id: group.refusal.pane,
            kind: 'refusal',
            label: group.refusal.label,
            missing: group.refusal.missing,
            note: group.refusal.note,
            reason,
        });
    }
    // A window with nothing in it is skipped. Measured on the built site: the
    // frameworks window fills on all 40 blocks while only 3 of them provide a slot of
    // it, so testing the slots alone is what kept the data panes invisible.
    if (panes.length === 0) continue;
    rendered.push({ id: spec.id, title: spec.title ?? title, panes });
}
---

{/* One window per KIND, each shaped like a GNOME Workbench session. The first
    names the WIDGET and runs it; the rest name what they are — so the reference
    link goes on window 0, the one carrying the widget's own title. */}
<div class="adw-widget">
    {
        rendered.map((win, index) => (
            <AdwWidgetWindow
                title={win.title}
                refHref={index === 0 ? refHref : null}
                widget={title}
                panes={win.panes}
                padding={padding}
                scroll={scroll}
                stage={stage}
            />
        ))
    }
</div>

<script>
    // Registers every <adw-*> custom element — both the tab bar of this
    // component AND the live-preview widgets slotted into it. This static import
    // runs before the code below, so every element is defined by the time the
    // previews mount.
    import '@gjsify/adwaita-web';
    // The element CLASS, for the ordinal door below. `<adw-tab-view>` has no settable
    // ordinal any more (ADR 0048) and an attribute is a string no type sees, so the
    // selection has to go through a method a type can miss.
    import type { Adw } from '@gjsify/adwaita-web';

    // Mount each preview: clone its inert <template> into the stage now that the
    // custom elements are defined. Building the subtree and attaching it in one go
    // (rather than letting the parser upgrade it in place) avoids the
    // nested-composite upgrade-order race.
    for (const tpl of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLTemplateElement>('.adw-widget-preview-tpl')) {
        const stage = tpl.parentElement?.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.adw-widget-preview-stage');
        if (!stage || stage.dataset.mounted) continue;
        stage.dataset.mounted = '1';
        stage.append(tpl.content.cloneNode(true));
    }

    const KEY = 'gjsify-preferred-impl';

    const applyPreference = (impl: string | null, skip?: Element) => {
        if (!impl) return;
        for (const el of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-impl-tabs]')) {
            // The MARKED view, not just any: a mounted preview can itself be an
            // `<adw-tab-view>` — the Adw.TabView block on /adwaita/view-switching/ is
            // one — and an unmarked `querySelector` handed that one back and silently
            // retargeted it whenever the reader picked an implementation on any other
            // widget. The marker is what makes the read right rather than lucky, and
            // the preview window is now the case that proves it: that window has no
            // tab view of its own at all, so an unmarked selector there returns the
            // MOUNTED WIDGET and nothing else.
            const view = el.querySelector<Adw.TabView>('adw-tab-view[data-impl-view]');
            if (!view || view === skip) continue;
            const impls = (el.dataset.impls ?? '').split(',');
            const index = impls.indexOf(impl);
            // The windows hold disjoint pane sets, so a stored choice simply does not
            // apply to most of them: picking "Blueprint" moves every GJS window and
            // leaves the others where the reader left them.
            // A CALL, not an attribute: `selected="n"` was the door until ADR 0048 renamed
            // the markup half to `selected-page="<page id>"`, and these panes declare no
            // `page-id`. The stored preference is a NAME resolved to a position against the
            // list as it is now — nothing here holds the number, which is the whole
            // difference the ADR draws.
            if (index >= 0) view.selectNthPage(index);
        }
    };

    // Follow user selection: persist + mirror to every other widget on the page.
    document.addEventListener('notify::selected-page', (event) => {
        const view = event.target as HTMLElement;
        // ONLY this component's own implementation tab view. `notify::selected-page`
        // BUBBLES, and every widget preview lives inside the same `[data-impl-tabs]`
        // host, so an unguarded listener also caught the previews. On
        // /adwaita/view-switching/ the Adw.TabView preview IS an `<adw-tab-view>`:
        // clicking its "Details" chip arrived here as `detail.selected = 1`, was read
        // as "the reader picked implementation 1" (Blueprint), persisted, and rewrote
        // every widget on the page. The widgets above then swapped a long snippet for
        // a short one and shrank, so the content under the reader's cursor jumped
        // upward while scrollY never moved. Reported as "clicking a tab scrolls the
        // page", which is exactly what it looks like.
        if (!view?.matches?.('[data-impl-view]')) return;
        const host = view.closest('[data-impl-tabs]') as HTMLElement | null;
        if (!host) return;
        const impls = (host.dataset.impls ?? '').split(',');
        const impl = impls[(event as CustomEvent).detail?.selected ?? 0];
        if (!impl) return;
        try {
            localStorage.setItem(KEY, impl);
        } catch {
            // Private mode — selection just won't persist.
        }
        applyPreference(impl, view);
    });

    // Headerbar copy button → forward to the ACTIVE page's hidden Expressive
    // Code copy button (EC handles the clipboard; the Adwaita toast listens on
    // that button's click).
    const EC_COPY = '.copy button, .adw-code-headerbar-end button';
    document.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
        const btn = (event.target as HTMLElement).closest?.('.command-tabs-copy') as HTMLElement | null;
        if (!btn) return;
        const host = btn.closest('[data-impl-tabs]');
        // `.adw-tab-page` is applied by <adw-tab-view> as it adopts its pages, so
        // a window with a single pane — no tab view — has none, and the pane is the
        // window's own child.
        const active = host?.querySelector('.adw-tab-page:not([hidden]), .adw-widget-window-pane');
        // THE ACTIVE PANE ONLY. There used to be a fallback to the window's first code
        // block, for the live preview pane, which held a running widget and no code
        // while sharing a window with the markup that painted it. It shares no window
        // with code any more — and a REFUSAL pane now does: on a frameworks window
        // carrying Solid/Vue/React plus "Why no XML", a fallback would answer a press
        // on the prose pane by copying a snippet the reader is not looking at. A press
        // with nothing to copy does nothing and marks nothing, which is what a window
        // with no code at all already did (see `copyable` in AdwWidgetWindow).
        const ecButton = active?.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>(EC_COPY);
        if (!ecButton) return;
        ecButton.click();
        btn.classList.add('copied');
        setTimeout(() => btn.classList.remove('copied'), 1500);
    });

    // Initial: restore the remembered implementation once the element is defined.
    customElements.whenDefined('adw-tab-view').then(() => {
        try {
            applyPreference(localStorage.getItem(KEY));
        } catch {
            // ignore
        }
    });
</script>

<style is:global>
    .adw-widget {
        margin-top: 1.5rem;
    }

    /* One window per kind, stacked. They are separate windows, so they are spaced
       like windows rather than joined like the panes inside one. */
    .adw-widget-window + .adw-widget-window {
        margin-top: 0.75rem;
    }

    /* ── A window with ONE pane ─────────────────────────────────────────
          No tab bar, so no <adw-tab-view> and none of its surface either: this
          stands in for `.adw-tab-view-pages`, the box a tabbed window's panes sit
          in, so a lone pane is painted on the same view. `.command-tabs` clips at
          radius 12px, so there is no corner to round here. */
    .adw-widget-window-pane {
        background-color: var(--view-bg-color);
        color: var(--view-fg-color);
        overflow: hidden;
    }

    /* ── The header bar's trailing slot ─────────────────────────────────
       Reserved whether or not the copy button is in it. A window whose panes
       hold no code drops the button (AdwWidgetWindow's `copyable`), and the
       trailing span is `flex-shrink: 0` with no width of its own — so an empty
       one collapses from 40px to its 6px padding and the centred title of that
       window slides 17px left of the titles stacked above and below it. Same
       width, both ways: 34px button plus its 3px padding on each side. */
    .adw-widget-window .command-tabs-headerbar-end {
        box-sizing: border-box;
        width: 40px;
    }

    /* ── Live preview pane ──────────────────────────────────────────────
       The only pane of the window that runs the widget: an Adwaita "view"
       surface the real widget is painted onto, directly under the header bar. It
       carries no frame and no separator of its own — the window around it owns
       every edge, and the header bar above it is the only line the reader needs. */
    .adw-widget-preview {
        background: var(--adw-view-bg);
        overflow: hidden;
        /* Subtle GNOME-wallpaper-ish tint so a white row/card stays visible on
           the white view surface, mirroring the widget gallery's backdrop. */
        background-image:
            radial-gradient(circle at 0% 0%, rgba(53, 132, 228, 0.06), transparent 45%),
            radial-gradient(circle at 100% 100%, rgba(146, 110, 228, 0.05), transparent 45%);
    }

    :root[data-theme='dark'] .adw-widget-preview {
        background-color: var(--adw-window-bg);
        background-image:
            radial-gradient(circle at 0% 0%, rgba(120, 174, 237, 0.08), transparent 45%),
            radial-gradient(circle at 100% 100%, rgba(146, 110, 228, 0.08), transparent 45%);
    }

    /* A modal dialog paints a position:fixed scrim covering its containing
       block. A transform on the preview panel makes the panel that containing
       block, so an opened dialog stays inside the preview instead of covering
       the whole page. Scoped to previews that actually hold a dialog. */
    .adw-widget-preview:has(adw-alert-dialog, adw-about-dialog, adw-preferences-dialog, adw-dialog) {
        transform: translateZ(0);
    }

    /* The box the widget is mounted into. The `stage` prop adds an inline style
       here: the size a doc page has to give a widget that has none, kept out of
       the markup the reader copies. */
    .adw-widget-preview-stage {
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        justify-content: center;
        min-height: 132px;
    }

    .adw-widget-preview.is-comfortable .adw-widget-preview-stage {
        padding: 28px 24px;
    }

    .adw-widget-preview.is-flush .adw-widget-preview-stage {
        padding: 0;
        align-items: stretch;
    }

    .adw-widget-preview.is-scroll .adw-widget-preview-stage {
        justify-content: flex-start;
        overflow-x: auto;
    }

    /* Narrow viewports: stop stretching the tabs to equal widths and let the bar
       scroll instead — which is what Adw.TabBar itself does when its tabs no
       longer fit. Equal widths on a phone gave each of four tabs about 80px, and
       a label then ellipsises down to something that names nothing. The bar
       already has `overflow-x: auto`; it just needs tabs that keep their own
       width. */
    @media (max-width: 700px) {
        .adw-widget-window adw-tab-view[expand-tabs] .adw-tab {
            flex: 0 0 auto;
            white-space: nowrap;
        }
    }

    /* The preview widgets are light-DOM custom elements styled by the global
       adwaita-web stylesheet; keep them from stretching full width so small
       widgets stay centered and legible.

       NOT adw-clamp, which was in this list. `_clamp.scss` keeps that element
       uncapped and full-width on purpose: it is the box the tightening curve is
       measured against, and the cap it applies goes on its CHILDREN. Capped at
       420px it clamped a 400px child inside a 420px box, so the Layout page
       promised "it stops growing past the maximum size and stays centred" over
       20px in which to show either. */
    .adw-widget-preview-stage > :where(adw-preferences-group, adw-banner) {
        width: 100%;
        max-width: 420px;
    }

    /* ── Reference link — a flat CSD button in the (otherwise empty) left slot
          of the tab-window headerbar, so the title stays centered. Opens the
          widget's canonical upstream docs. ── */
    .command-tabs .command-tabs-headerbar-start {
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        justify-content: flex-start;
        padding-left: 3px;
    }

    .command-tabs-ref {
        display: inline-flex;
        align-items: center;
        justify-content: center;
        width: 34px;
        height: 34px;
        border-radius: 9px;
        color: inherit;
        opacity: 0.65;
        transition:
            background 0.1s ease,
            opacity 0.1s ease;
    }

    .command-tabs-ref:hover {
        background: rgba(128, 128, 128, 0.12);
        opacity: 1;
    }

    .command-tabs-ref:active {
        background: rgba(128, 128, 128, 0.22);
    }

    .command-tabs-ref-icon {
        display: block;
        width: 16px;
        height: 16px;
        background-color: currentColor;
        -webkit-mask: url("data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'%20width='16'%20height='16'%20viewBox='0%200%2016%2016'%20fill='none'%20stroke='%23000'%20stroke-width='1.4'%20stroke-linecap='round'%20stroke-linejoin='round'%3E%3Cpath%20d='M6.5%203.5H3.2A1.2%201.2%200%200%200%202%204.7v8.1A1.2%201.2%200%200%200%203.2%2014h8.1a1.2%201.2%200%200%200%201.2-1.2V9.5'/%3E%3Cpath%20d='M9.5%202H14v4.5'/%3E%3Cpath%20d='M7%209%2014%202'/%3E%3C/svg%3E") no-repeat center / contain;
        mask: url("data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'%20width='16'%20height='16'%20viewBox='0%200%2016%2016'%20fill='none'%20stroke='%23000'%20stroke-width='1.4'%20stroke-linecap='round'%20stroke-linejoin='round'%3E%3Cpath%20d='M6.5%203.5H3.2A1.2%201.2%200%200%200%202%204.7v8.1A1.2%201.2%200%200%200%203.2%2014h8.1a1.2%201.2%200%200%200%201.2-1.2V9.5'/%3E%3Cpath%20d='M9.5%202H14v4.5'/%3E%3Cpath%20d='M7%209%2014%202'/%3E%3C/svg%3E") no-repeat center / contain;
    }
